  "headlines": [
    {
      "title": "Rolling monthly contracts are the biggest leak",
      "detail": "48% of month-to-month customers leave, against 14% of those on a two-year deal - about 3 times higher. Nothing is holding the monthly customer in place, so every bill is a chance to reconsider."
    },
    {
      "title": "The first six months decide everything",
      "detail": "50% of customers churn inside their first six months, falling to 20% once they pass four years. Attention paid early is worth far more than a discount offered late."
    },
    {
      "title": "The premium product loses the most customers",
      "detail": "Fibre optic users leave at 48% versus 26% on slower DSL, even though fibre costs more. People paying a premium expect a premium experience, and judge it harshly."
    },
    {
      "title": "How they pay reveals how committed they are",
      "detail": "Electronic-cheque payers churn at 50%, while the safest automatic payment group sits at 17%. Someone who has set up autopay has quietly decided to stay."
    },
    {
      "title": "Bundled customers are harder to lose",
      "detail": "Customers who stay use 4.5 services on average; those who leave use 4.1. Each extra service is one more thing to unpick before switching provider."
    },
    {
      "title": "What all this is worth",
      "detail": "The 16 customers who left were paying $1,174 a month - $14,092 a year walking out of the door. Keeping even a third of them pays for the whole retention effort."
    }
